  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "v3.5.0 — Audit logging: the records that answer \"who did what, to what, and with what outcome\" now have a path step-up gating can never drop. Added: `AddAuditLogging<TSink>()`, `IAuditLogger<T>` with `AuditAsync(AuditEvent)` and an optional companion log, the `AuditEvent` record, and `IAuditEventSink`. Writes bypass Serilog entirely and go straight to your sink, so the write is awaited and a sink failure propagates to the business call site instead of vanishing into a void return. The package ships no `IAuditEventSink` implementation by design — a default that mirrors to logs or silently swallows records manufactures false confidence in a trail nobody checked; the README carries a database-backed one to copy. Audit is turned off by not calling `AddAuditLogging`; there is no configuration flag. `AddAuditLogging` requires `AddStepUpLogging` (audit resolves the client IP by the same rule as request logging) and fails at host start with a message naming both methods, in either call order, so a deployment that forgot it never serves a request that should have been audited. Two counters under the `StepUpLogging.Audit` meter: `audit_events_total{outcome}` and `audit_write_failures_total`. Fixes #19. Nothing changes for consumers who do not call `AddAuditLogging`. No public API break.",
